#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069687} :activity/id #uuid "30066841-ab6e-53c6-9a38-adfdeaaa83f8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048243 :topic/id #uuid "8b9ecafe-ee3d-5fbf-845a-d0ba5868f663" :topic/math-academy-id 413} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069687 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069918 :tutorial/content "Consider the finite region bounded by the curve\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nthe $x$-axis, and the vertical line $x=2$, as shown in the picture below.\n\n![](images/35/35633369d6f4a31ae4c62bb8830dafd9a849aefe434286e2dbf071b91539ccd6.png)\n\nWhen we rotate this region $360^\\circ$ about the $x$-axis (called the **axis of revolution**), we get a **solid of revolution,** as shown below. How can we find the volume of this solid?\n\n![](images/e2/e214876a70bdce94316e72d362ebb985bf8e20eaecb90fc427b33c814e647d70.png)\n\nTo find the volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by a curve $y=f(x)$, the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=a$ and $x=b$ is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis, we use the following formula:\n\n$$\nV = \\pi \\int_{a}^{b} y^2 \\:\\textrm{d}x = \\pi \\int_{a}^{b} \\left[ f(x) \\right]^2 \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThe method corresponding to the formula above is called the **disc method** because it involves breaking up the region into many thin discs (or cylinders) of height $\\Delta{x}$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/02/0214ae60481f0bfa5d1fee7526b6fba48dfe1ee3cba39329e790ca1db79f5dc4.png)\n\nWhen $\\Delta{x} \\to 0$, the radius of each disc can be considered equal to $y=f(x)$. So, the volume of the disc (cylinder) is\n\n$$\n\\pi y^2 \\Delta{x} = \\pi \\left[ f(x) \\right]^2 \\Delta{x}\n$$\n\nIntegrating this expression from $a$ to $b$, we obtain the formula for the volume of the solid.\n\nIn our example above, we have\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nso the volume is given by\n\n$$\nV = \\pi\\int_0^2 (\\sqrt{x})^2\\,\\textrm d x = \\pi\\int_0^2 x\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nEvaluating this integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= π \\cdot \\left.\\frac{x^{2}}{2}\\right|_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= π(\\frac{2^{2}}{2} - \\frac{0^{2}}{2}) \\\\\n&= 2π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the volume of the solid is $2\\pi$ cubic units." :tutorial/id #uuid "7fb01e7d-02e7-509a-9164-3bc3410ec61b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1049 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a506c8a7-fa07-5d41-a0cc-8b8d8195ddc9" :step/math-academy-id 2062 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069688}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069688 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068774 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068616 :question/id #uuid "53763f9f-33d7-589b-8267-3c28cfa95f4f" :question/math-academy-id "e-587"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11acd688-485b-582e-b6e0-78a8db379da3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049225}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069403}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About the X-Axis"} :step/id #uuid "fcf1f892-2742-548b-ac12-a3e96bdc7e93" :step/math-academy-id 2063 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069710}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069689 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069919 :tutorial/content "Consider the finite region bounded by the curve $x=y^2$, the $y$-axis, and the horizontal line $y=1$, as shown in the picture below.\n\n![](images/63/63af72dd7ad6540e418a0947f9c4934a05ade6257904f9eb63733d035a776de8.png)\n\nWhen we rotate this region $360^\\circ$ about the $y$-axis, we again get a solid of revolution, as shown below. How can we find the volume of this solid?\n\n![](images/67/67896ee0cc1445194000c0415bbd4453fe93d7cc136cc7479a32df3205d71b5a.png)\n\nTo find the volume of this solid, we can use the same *disc method* approach that we used earlier for the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/27/2730269b09e2019c3f45e48a9dc7849260426dd7ecf1103da74f62f04280f902.png)\n\nThe only difference is that we need to swap $x$ and $y$. The radius of each disc (cylinder) is equal to $x=g(y)$ and its height is $\\Delta{y}$. So, the volume of the disc (cylinder) is\n\n$$\n\\pi x^2 \\Delta{y} = \\pi [ g(y) ]^2 \\Delta{y}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to integrate this expression. Therefore, to find the volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by a curve $x=g(y)$, the $y$-axis, and the horizontal lines $y=c$ and $y=d$ is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis, we use the following formula:\n\n$$\nV = \\pi \\int_{c}^{d} \\left[ g(y) \\right]^2 \\:\\textrm{d}y\n$$\n\nIn our example above, we have $x=y^2$, so the volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= \\int_{0}^{1}[y^{2}]^{2}dy = π\\int_{0}^{1}y^{4}dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating this integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= π\\int_{0}^{1}y^{4}dy \\\\\n&= π \\cdot \\left.\\frac{y^{5}}{5}\\right|_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= π(\\frac{1}{5} - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "003d7555-c1b0-563e-bd03-4ef97f59c338" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1051 :tutorial/title "Rotation About the Y-Axis"} :step/id #uuid "4f3a7848-a23f-5d5d-b7a4-82faf2c0945c" :step/math-academy-id 2067 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069711}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069710 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068789 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068633 :question/id #uuid "a50a4a48-b7d5-5938-8f20-db5d7a1f31b1" :question/math-academy-id "e-6943"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67695100-6401-55c8-8868-f3fe64a60a37" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069103} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069260}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About the X-Axis"} :step/id #uuid "2c8b7076-cade-5332-a857-ceadf135feaf" :step/math-academy-id 25639 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069689}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069711 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068790 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068634 :question/id #uuid "ec270f3b-a2f5-51d1-aef7-19c37ac8fa5d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6944"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b483692-6b19-5ef3-9e00-de1611e59977" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050393}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069104} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527995}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About the Y-Axis"} :step/id #uuid "b852805d-8608-5303-8d68-a2e4f0c069dc" :step/math-academy-id 25642 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069716}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069716 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068794 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068638 :question/id #uuid "a25b6d87-7779-5408-90d6-a558baaae70f" :question/math-academy-id "e-7055"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eb97de2-747b-5888-b813-c411fe755a30" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069013} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069105}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About the Y-Axis"} :step/id #uuid "064b798b-0025-54c6-b5a7-7143c7eb42da" :step/math-academy-id 26013}] :activity/title "Volumes of Revolution Using the Disc Method: Rotation About the Coordinate Axes" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068948}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065063} :activity/id #uuid "6a8803f8-cdad-59a2-a9d6-fe10c0d001fc" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048403 :topic/id #uuid "9c41899e-334d-5c90-83f2-ff0d4e02a917" :topic/math-academy-id 316} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065063 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065432 :tutorial/content "At first sight, the integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int x \\sqrt{x+2} \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nmight not seem like it can be solved using substitution because $x$ is not the derivative of $x+2.$ But in fact, with some clever manipulation, we can indeed solve it using substitution!\n\nSetting\n\n$$\nu=x+2,\n$$\n\nand computing the required derivatives gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int x \\sqrt{x+2} \\,\\textrm{d}x=\\int x \\sqrt{u} \\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nSo we still have an $x$ in our integrand. However, we can get rid of it by solving for $x$ in the rule $u=x+2.$ We get\n\n$$\nx=u-2,\n$$\n\nand using this, the integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u= & \\int (u-2)\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int (u-2){u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve the integral by multiplying out the product and integrating the resulting function with respect to $u\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (u-2){u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\int ({u}^{3/2}-2{u}^{1/2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}u-2\\int {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}{u}^{5/2}-\\frac{4}{3}{u}^{3/2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the final step, we substitute $u=x+2,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\sqrt{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{2}{5}(x+2)^{5/2}-\\frac{4}{3}(x+2)^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\sqrt{(x+2)^{5}}-\\frac{4}{3}\\sqrt{(x+2)^{3}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6d16cf43-c7f8-5600-82a2-55a301cc5a4f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 843 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "cdacec4c-760a-523e-bd19-189115482c57" :step/math-academy-id 1626 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065065}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065064 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064209 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064080 :question/id #uuid "109a6d6e-8e62-5eac-bfa8-7c059399efe8" :question/math-academy-id "e-457"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "94ad8efa-33ee-5451-b6b9-c99cb81a9ba2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048332}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064493} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064497} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284633} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319095} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319096} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319239} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332733} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332734} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392689} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528003}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Definite Integrals of Algebraic Functions Using Substitution"} :step/id #uuid "04eb5284-3202-50f0-b91f-023a0a6f28a8" :step/math-academy-id 1627} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065065 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064210 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064081 :question/id #uuid "a40f6e7e-f4d3-5f3e-bf9d-99c640e36274" :question/math-academy-id "e-458"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2feb2115-bd75-5c09-87cc-f10cf46c182f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050771}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064860} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259761} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292607} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292610} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292611} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323705} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323708} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332708} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332732}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals of Algebraic Functions Using Substitution"} :step/id #uuid "2e6e3820-c15f-5755-b9de-b18643215e43" :step/math-academy-id 1631 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065064}}] :activity/title "Further Integration of Algebraic Functions Using Substitution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064412}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069630} :activity/id #uuid "ad5bc873-c059-5e29-a890-6a3c7109ea97" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048705 :topic/id #uuid "b6d4a471-5c94-57bb-ac87-9fd114198d79" :topic/math-academy-id 424} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069630 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070032 :tutorial/content "In some cases, applying integration by parts can lead to a cyclic pattern, where the original integral reappears after a few steps. This cyclic behavior allows us to solve the integral by forming an equation that can be solved algebraically.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the following example:\n\n$$\nI = \\int \\dfrac{\\ln x}{x} \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nLet's try using the method of integration by parts. We suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}x & \\,\\Rightarrow \\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x} & \\,\\Rightarrow \\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}x(\\operatorname{ln}x)-\\int (\\operatorname{ln}x)(\\frac{1}{x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{2}x-\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}x}{x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{2}x-I\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have the same integral $I$ on both sides of the equality. Thinking about $I$ as some unknown variable, we can easily solve this equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={ln}^{2}x-I \\\\ I+I & ={ln}^{2}x \\\\ 2I & ={ln}^{2}x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{ln}^{2}x}{2}+C\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we added a constant of integration right at the very end of the calculation." :tutorial/id #uuid "eac7b2d2-bdab-56e5-bb03-00e69bb0caf6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 904 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ebba75b0-acff-50bb-8bc5-876149ab4775" :step/math-academy-id 1752 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069631}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069631 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068734 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068580 :question/id #uuid "5b83575b-9674-5dc5-b3eb-a918f145be93" :question/math-academy-id "e-497"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "df0c3b59-7667-53e9-84d3-bba30ea116fb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049332}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069040} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250330} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319301}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Logarithmic Expression Using Cyclic Integration by Parts"} :step/id #uuid "47b51ee2-f969-5eae-b3ba-5c4a58e33b36" :step/math-academy-id 1753 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069632}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069632 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068735 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068581 :question/id #uuid "72d0b4b3-bfc6-5339-809f-ff7cd63e4d3a" :question/math-academy-id "e-498"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b428331b-4916-5b2c-8aa6-89a2358cdef0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050101} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050489} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050789}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069068} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275319} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275320} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314828} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314857} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332751}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Indefinite Integral of a Exponential-Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts"} :step/id #uuid "08653810-09fe-5a4e-9e3a-e82085484170" :step/math-academy-id 1757 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069725}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069725 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068801 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068644 :question/id #uuid "f415cae9-6b18-53c1-8c47-6e0a1f0de59c" :question/math-academy-id "e-7313"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1da25ef-5d1b-588f-b1cc-121562ff98ea" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319311} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323781} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323782} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332792} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332793} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351476} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351477} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351478}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Definite Integral of an Exponential-Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts"} :step/id #uuid "3694456d-b1db-5517-9b35-d043cdc3d3d6" :step/math-academy-id 27070}] :activity/title "Integration by Parts in Cyclic Cases" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068957}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065139} :activity/id #uuid "ec92f9e5-f4ff-5e7d-bf06-75063f0fdeca" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048771 :topic/id #uuid "bd590864-b974-5af1-ab3d-4de57a31118a" :topic/math-academy-id 311} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065139 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065333 :tutorial/content "Let's find the total area of the finite region enclosed between curves the curves\n\n$$\nf(x)=x^3-2x\n$$\n\nand $g(x)=2x$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e6/e651f9df1fbc9f0f9ad8ffa49822e4e3640a3555570ca3d0f82c155c5141843e.png)\n\nFirst, we find the points where the curves intersect:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= g(x) \\\\\nx^{3} - 2x &= 2x \\\\\nx^{3} - 4x &= 0 \\\\\nx(x^{2} - 4) &= 0 \\\\\nx(x - 2)(x + 2) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 0, 2,-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area we want consists of two symmetric regions: $A_1$ and $A_2$.\n\n![](images/4f/4fd6173127725bd771a753217002ab9656cf36a97dd37c4fe80f7d76e672ee86.png)\n\nFor the area $A_1$, we have $x \\in [-2,0]$, and the upper function is $f(x)$ while the lower function is $g(x)$. So, the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{1} &= \\int_{-2}^{0}(f(x) - g(x))dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{-2}^{0}((x^{3} - 2x) - 2x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{-2}^{0}(x^{3} - 4x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{-2}^{0}x^{3}dx - 4\\int_{-2}^{0}xdx \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{x^{4}}{4}\\right|_{-2}^{0} - 4 \\cdot \\left.\\frac{x^{2}}{2}\\right|_{-2}^{0} \\\\\n&= (0 - 4) - 4(0 - 2) \\\\\n&=-4 + 8 \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the regions are symmetric, we have that $A_2=A_1=4$.\n\nFinally, the required area is given by\n\n$$\nA = A_1 + A_2 = 4 + 4 = 8\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "988c723b-81d1-541c-9ab3-bebbece1cf06" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1003 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3d1d4c5b-f669-5ef8-ae3a-069d89747d69" :step/math-academy-id 1966 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065178}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065140 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064260 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064105 :question/id #uuid "2e0348cd-6ee1-5e95-8164-2edc58084312" :question/math-academy-id "e-558"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebf33bf5-1693-5ffa-b545-379f5dc60622" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064885} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064886}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area Between Two Curves Expressed as Functions of X That Intersect at Three Points"} :step/id #uuid "0b4e1339-dcb7-5439-a531-66ef32ae8045" :step/math-academy-id 1967 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065141}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065141 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064261 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064106 :question/id #uuid "a36dbf41-414c-55e3-a9f6-4b925d571442" :question/math-academy-id "e-559"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf150ae8-865f-5eda-8725-731d4e915873" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050548}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064882} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527929}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area Between Two Curves Expressed as Functions of Y That Intersect at Three Points"} :step/id #uuid "91fabaf9-366a-5699-bd24-1f3331ce4790" :step/math-academy-id 1971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065178 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064289 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064130 :question/id #uuid "2a359419-c37d-5772-bf4b-1f2757272ad9" :question/math-academy-id "e-7357"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f2ec03d-c811-5009-8498-7b5960bc8d0f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049042} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050827}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064881} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064884}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Integral Expression That Represents the Area Between Two Curves"} :step/id #uuid "55aedc57-c528-5f39-b05b-126f9ef5cee0" :step/math-academy-id 27313 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065140}}] :activity/title "Finding Areas Between Curves that Intersect at More Than Two Points" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064407}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176692} :activity/id #uuid "def4663c-59d2-5447-81ce-d279fab17096" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048837 :topic/id #uuid "c4d057b8-12ac-5f2a-94f7-d0b239050514" :topic/math-academy-id 3560} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176674 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175948 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175805 :question/id #uuid "7d2aa3e0-f6de-5e11-95d8-8877a87b13db" :question/math-academy-id "e-749"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfc0d9e1-bce1-5d62-b3c8-f7d7bb226497" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050624}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176358} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176363} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244100} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244295} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269178} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319185} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320892} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320893} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399384}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius and Interval of Convergence of a Power Series"} :step/id #uuid "6d91432f-0bfa-58bd-808c-9a2779a7ca28" :step/math-academy-id 2557 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176683}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176683 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175956 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175802 :question/id #uuid "7b5adbc9-3fa0-5388-8b62-9053147fbfe5" :question/math-academy-id "e-7227"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "60f61c26-ff2d-5343-becd-d8f44f623cd3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176507} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237976} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237977} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332833} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332834} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332835} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332836} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332837}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Convergence of a Power Series"} :step/id #uuid "e98c072b-028e-5af9-99ec-59557e741afb" :step/math-academy-id 26731} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176692 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176978 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have the following infinite power series.\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{(x-2)^n}{n^2} = (x-2) + \\dfrac{(x-2)^2}{2^2} + \\dfrac{(x-2)^3}{3^2} + \\cdots\n$$\n\nHow do we determine the radius and interval of convergence for this series?\n\nFirst, note that the terms of the series are all powers of $(x-2).$ For this reason, we say that the series is **centered** at $x=2.$\n\nTo compute the radius of convergence, we use the ratio test for convergence. We define \n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{(x-2)^n}{n^2},\n$$\n\nand we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(x-2)^{n+1}}{(n+1)^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{{n}^{2}}{(x-2)^{n}}| \\\\  & =|x-2|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{n}^{2}}{(n+1)^{2}}) \\\\  & =|x-2|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{n}^{2}}{{n}^{2}+2n+1}) \\\\  & =|x-2|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{(1+\\frac{2}{n}+\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}})}) \\\\  & =|x-2|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =|x-2|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if \n\n$$\nL < {\\color{blue}1}\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\left| x-{\\color{red}2} \\right| < {\\color{blue}1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n- the radius of convergence is ${\\color{blue}1},$ and\n\n- the interval of convergence is centered at the point $x={\\color{red}2}.$\n\nTo find the interval of convergence, we solve the inequality $\\left| x-2 \\right| < 1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x-2| & <1 \\\\ -1<x-2 & <1 \\\\ 1<x & <3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the series converges for any $x$ in the open interval $(1,3).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series at the endpoints of this interval. So, we need to investigate the endpoints $x=1$ and $x=3$ separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- We start with the left endpoint. When $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x-2)^{n}}{{n}^{2}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(1-2)^{n}}{{n}^{2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}} \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{1}{16}+⋯,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test.\n\n- We then look at the right endpoint. When $x=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x-2)^{n}}{{n}^{2}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(3-2)^{n}}{{n}^{2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{1}^{n}}{{n}^{2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{1}{16}+⋯,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the $p$-series test.\n\nFinally, we conclude that the interval of convergence is $I=[1,3].$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3711e5cb-ff53-5f13-a62a-da24f59e888b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5191 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "638d27e8-a48f-5883-adc8-de19b25a5a0e" :step/math-academy-id 32549 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176674}}] :activity/title "Radius of Convergence of Power Series" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176111}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101819} :activity/id #uuid "eca39ae6-43d5-5733-a009-55c6a59593e6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048856 :topic/id #uuid "c6b5949b-df61-5d36-8c55-055311c70a59" :topic/math-academy-id 1176} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101819 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186102012 :tutorial/content "Let's see a trick that can be used to compute the sum of the following series:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n = 1}^\\infty \\dfrac 1 {n (n + 1)}\n$$\n\nFirst, we can use partial fractions to write\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{n(n+1)} = \\dfrac{1}{n} - \\dfrac{1}{n+1}\n$$\n\nThen the sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n = 1}^\\infty \\dfrac 1 {n (n + 1)} = \\sum_{n = 1}^\\infty\\left(\\dfrac{1}{n} - \\dfrac{1}{n+1}\\right)\n$$\n\nComputing the first few partial sums, *without* simplifying, notice that many terms cancel:\n\n$\\require{cancel}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ns_{1} &= (1- \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\\ns_{2} &= (1 - \\frac{1}{2}) + (\\frac{1}{2}- \\frac{1}{3}) \\\\\ns_{3} &= (1 - \\frac{1}{2}) + (\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{3}) + (\\frac{1}{3}- \\frac{1}{4}) \\\\\ns_{4} &= (1 - \\frac{1}{2}) + (\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{3}) + (\\frac{1}{3} - \\frac{1}{4}) + (\\frac{1}{4}- \\frac{1}{5})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor each partial sum, we always end up canceling all terms except for the first and last terms. We say that this infinite series is a **telescoping series**, meaning that it consists of only a finite number of terms after canceling.\n\nSo, if $s_N$ is the $N$th partial sum of the series, we have\n\n$$\ns_N = {\\color{blue}{1}} {\\color{red}{\\,-\\, \\dfrac 1 {N + 1}}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can determine the sum of the series by computing the limit of $s_N$ as $N\\to\\infty$. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(1)/(n(n + 1)) &= lim_(N → ∞)(1 - \\frac{1}{N + 1}) \\\\\n&= 1 - 0 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7785a1f9-f6fd-5dac-bbab-9da2b149e341" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6768 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f09ad925-45f1-583f-9d96-f7db4c4c6d03" :step/math-academy-id 41059 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101820}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101820 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100965 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100583 :question/id #uuid "f2af7002-adda-5491-9747-11672a1e47e5" :question/math-academy-id "e-10834"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b8750bd-210d-5da2-aeb2-83fd615d979d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050050} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050706}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101491} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101492}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Sum of a Series Whose Denominator Is the Product of N and N+1"} :step/id #uuid "1d7a95a6-44a2-55cb-a4bc-c0ee15b8cece" :step/math-academy-id 41060 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101821}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101821 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100966 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100584 :question/id #uuid "278de328-de74-51ef-a6e6-380a99dbcd95" :question/math-academy-id "e-10835"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c034fbc0-b497-53f2-8e9c-9d12afa04ec2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101457} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101458}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Sum of a Series Whose Denominator Is the Product of Two Consecutive Expressions"} :step/id #uuid "053b72df-f735-53b4-9d30-87093c9b677a" :step/math-academy-id 41063 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101822}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101822 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100967 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100585 :question/id #uuid "36d7c5e4-2d11-55cd-98da-3f9d43d46125" :question/math-academy-id "e-10836"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7f1114c-8155-5578-8e1d-0ba8fc010157" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101547} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101548}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Sum of a Series Whose Denominator Is the Product of Two Non-Consecutive Expressions (x+a)(x+a+2)"} :step/id #uuid "aaec4c89-d6c7-590f-98d3-134abf397033" :step/math-academy-id 41066}] :activity/title "Telescoping Series" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186101042}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106952} :activity/id #uuid "e36ee21c-371b-5637-8539-624dc138ba57" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048880 :topic/id #uuid "c9449775-49cd-5723-869d-965280e34916" :topic/math-academy-id 1382} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106952 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107173 :tutorial/content "Consider an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty, \\infty),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\int_{-\\infty}^{0} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x + \\int_{0}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent." :tutorial/id #uuid "b58359c2-9f54-5bd7-8005-df9313559853" :tutorial/math-academy-id 932 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e2a6a97c-d17d-5e17-9d7b-d0d1dbe224ca" :step/math-academy-id 618349 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106953}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106953 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105922 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105608 :question/id #uuid "dbd8b706-9ace-5adb-9c8e-29b0d21bfa82" :question/math-academy-id "e-515"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e93fd7f-070f-5359-aabb-cba5cc275015" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048115} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048618} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051015}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106493} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253501} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253504} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288010} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351298}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Convergent or Divergent Integrals With Unbounded Limits"} :step/id #uuid "61313614-0ee3-57e5-8e4b-da016cba1b7b" :step/math-academy-id 618350 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106954}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106954 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107099 :tutorial/content "Computing the definite integral of a function over the entire real line requires us to break the integral into two parts and separately evaluate each one.\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\int_{-\\infty}^{0} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x + \\int_{0}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nEvaluating two improper integrals can be quite time-consuming. However, we can shorten the process if we see that $f(x)$ is an even or odd function. \n\n- Recall that $f(x)$ is an even function if $f(-x) = f(x).$ Even functions are symmetrical about the $y$-axis. Therefore, if $f(x)$ is even, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{0} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\int_{0}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x,\n$$\n\nprovided that the integrals are convergent. Moreover, if $f(x)$ is even and the above integrals are convergent, then\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x = 2\\int_{0}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\n- Recall that $f(x)$ is an odd function if $f(-x) = -f(x).$ Therefore, if $f(x)$ is odd, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{0} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x = -\\int_{0}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x,\n$$\n\nprovided that the integrals are convergent. Moreover, if $f(x)$ is odd and the above integrals are convergent, then\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x = 0.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "04e63304-93d0-5edd-985c-6a732614917f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4359 :tutorial/title "Improper Integrals of Even and Odd Functions"} :step/id #uuid "4ed58d6e-1526-5297-982b-b32247e84e4b" :step/math-academy-id 618353 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106955}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106955 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105923 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105607 :question/id #uuid "705ea272-efb2-54f4-b527-c1d4305e0c6c" :question/math-academy-id "e-514"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fe1f468-9e8d-58fc-bd61-cfe1191a5a19" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049873}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106142} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253507} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288011} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288013} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319189} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319192} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320868} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527936}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Improper Integral With Unbounded Limits"} :step/id #uuid "647a58a8-d8cb-5567-85af-21033f2a72c6" :step/math-academy-id 618354 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106956}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106956 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105924 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105609 :question/id #uuid "aad39658-8d3f-5893-b7dd-603b62064c5e" :question/math-academy-id "e-516"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d083b53b-8c39-5785-a250-5649001e822b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048946}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106492} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253521} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288007} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320879} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332975}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area of an Unbounded Region"} :step/id #uuid "f45b6067-3889-5762-becb-da9ab2269834" :step/math-academy-id 618357}] :activity/title "Improper Integrals Over the Real Line" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186106007}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172979} :activity/id #uuid "dd0bd3a2-aeb8-58a5-aac7-31019071b253" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048885 :topic/id #uuid "c96ee93a-fd82-595c-824f-e11934066ffc" :topic/math-academy-id 3350} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172868 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172016 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171820 :question/id #uuid "8a1be39d-6621-5d71-b1a4-3669d210ee8e" :question/math-academy-id "e-6986"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3e3fdc8-67d0-5b46-9c6b-c1ec8d4ea24f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172564} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172570} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256382} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332816} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332817} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332818} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348439} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382964}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using Asymptotes"} :step/id #uuid "c590d4ee-83a9-5629-b80d-5b9bf0fef93a" :step/math-academy-id 25710} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172869 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172017 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171821 :question/id #uuid "f43aebf0-6aa9-58cf-9475-69f5af74d71f" :question/math-academy-id "e-6988"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1616167b-5679-5ff6-b385-3e1b59352c64" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048272}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172563} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172601} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253444} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332806} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348438}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using the Sign of the Slopes and Factoring"} :step/id #uuid "df729ab0-2123-5f4d-8bcf-9b5d2fb448d1" :step/math-academy-id 25712 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172868}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172870 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172018 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171822 :question/id #uuid "4dabeee5-1c46-5198-9c51-9099fb5df617" :question/math-academy-id "e-7126"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "244419de-9b13-5923-b0a5-1bdc6f9b03b2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172567} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172569} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253438} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253665} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253667} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332802} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332807}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using the Sign of the Slopes"} :step/id #uuid "cf643e99-f61d-5e66-8afd-c5e23c2c54df" :step/math-academy-id 26196 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172869}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172979 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173195 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the differential equation $y' = -4xy.$ The slope field for this equation is shown below.\n\n![](images/a4/a47ec9497553fdcc9d20607f309bdaee00bb1fc0af4d39dea8a6a843a2e417e6.png)\n\nLet's see how much information we can gather about the differential equation by referring *only* to its slope field.\n\n- First, note that the slope field has neither vertical nor horizontal symmetry. Therefore, the associated differential equation must be of the form $y' = f(x,y).$ Indeed, the equation $y' =-4xy$ is of this form.\n\n- Second, notice that the solution curves appear to have stationary points along the lines $x=0$ and $y=0.$ This means that the right-hand side of our differential equation must evaluate to zero on these lines. Indeed, this is true of our equation.\n\n![](images/7b/7beccfd6840c2715bfd3c923f4fe6ac05bdf07f9ed65931b016efd937b1a0b63.png)\n\n- Third, the lines $x=0$ and $y=0$ split the plane into four regions. The slopes of the solution curves in each of these regions are summarized in the table below.\n\n| $y > 0$ | $\\nearrow$\n\n$({\\color{blue}{+}})$ | $\\searrow$\n\n$({\\color{red}{-}})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y <       0$ | $\\searrow$\n\n$({\\color{red}{-}})$ | $\\nearrow$\n\n$({\\color{blue}{+}})$ |\n|  | $x<       0$ | $x> 0$ |\n\nNotice that the sign of $f(x,y)$ in the four regions agrees with the above table:\n\n- $f(x,y)=-4xy$ is positive in the second and fourth quadrants, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=-4xy$ is negative in the first and third quadrants." :tutorial/id #uuid "dd16b3e1-53b3-55bf-af84-540daaefde15" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5672 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9018b840-14fc-5403-a85b-0696a121416b" :step/math-academy-id 34802 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172982}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172982 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172082 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171888 :question/id #uuid "95746e3f-5cdf-58e2-8dbb-39b3cbe5cfc1" :question/math-academy-id "e-9356"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd82d96b-34d5-5d20-b810-7ea88b0b5797" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048489} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049722} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050480}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172566} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172568} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253664} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332800} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332801} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332803}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using Stationary Points"} :step/id #uuid "de33aa28-2c66-5a35-b972-8287ff822b59" :step/math-academy-id 35034 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172870}}] :activity/title "Analyzing Slope Fields for Nonautonomous Differential Equations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172172}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097588} :activity/id #uuid "85353624-7ac2-52d1-8562-1afe91b8759a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048976 :topic/id #uuid "d0827a60-1270-5e75-a5db-1a9836dc00b1" :topic/math-academy-id 1079} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097588 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097791 :tutorial/content "For a particle with position $x(t)$, the **average velocity** of the particle over the time interval $[a,b]$ is given by the average rate of change of position:\n\n$$\nv_{\\textrm{avg}} = \\dfrac{\\Delta x}{\\Delta t} = \\dfrac{x(b) - x(a)}{b-a}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, the **average acceleration** of a particle with velocity $v(t)$ over the time interval $[a,b]$ is given by the average rate of change of velocity:\n\n$$\na_{\\textrm{avg}} = \\dfrac{\\Delta v}{\\Delta t} = \\dfrac{v(b) - v(a)}{b-a}\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate, suppose that a particle moves along a straight line, and its position (in meters) relative to a fixed origin $O$ is given by the function\n\n$$\nx(t) = 2t^3\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nt \\geq 0\n$$\n\nis the time measured in seconds. The average velocity of the particle over the time interval $[0,1]$ is\n\n$$\nv_{avg} = \\frac{Δx}{Δt} = (x(1) - x(0))/(1 - 0) = (2(1)^{3} - 2(0)^{3})/(1 - 0) = 2m/s\n$$\n\nLikewise, the velocity is\n\n$$\nv(t) = x'(t) = 6t^2\n$$\n\nso the average acceleration of the particle over the time interval $[0,1]$ is\n\n$$\na_{avg} = \\frac{Δv}{Δt} = (v(1) - v(0))/(1 - 0) = (6(1)^{2} - 6(0)^{2})/(1 - 0) = 6m/s^{2}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e1c49e4b-7206-5819-b127-c2f562fa1043" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1023 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "defa2861-ea0b-5be8-a72a-4101e1177359" :step/math-academy-id 2011 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097589}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097589 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096717 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096517 :question/id #uuid "924d743e-4984-5d94-84be-381c99697940" :question/math-academy-id "e-571"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7a24f6a-aa0d-576a-a4ff-945335f3301b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050572} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050901}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097203} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097286}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Average Velocity of a Particle Given Its Position Function"} :step/id #uuid "c2c3e774-0466-5687-8348-ec20933d1013" :step/math-academy-id 2012 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097590}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097590 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096718 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096521 :question/id #uuid "523927ce-8e24-554d-8558-07f51a3d4120" :question/math-academy-id "e-572"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d62ff71-fe48-5612-8f37-bab7cb7f2931" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048713}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097285} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097291}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Average Acceleration of a Particle Given Its Velocity Function"} :step/id #uuid "9c935206-0fc0-512a-9bf5-4f346bee6bcf" :step/math-academy-id 2014 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097591}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097591 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097792 :tutorial/content "So, we now know how to find\n\n- the average *velocity* (given the *position*), and\n- the average *acceleration* (given the *velocity*).\n\nBut what if we are given the *velocity* and asked to find the average *velocity*? That's a bit trickier. However, we can solve this problem by recalling that the average value of a function $v(t)$ over the interval $[t_1,t_2]$ is given by\n\n$$\nv_{\\textrm{avg}} = \\dfrac{1}{t_2-t_1}\\int_{t_1}^{t_2} v(t)\\,\\textrm d t\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b25ff39d-cfec-5109-8c49-31a8d54ad75d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1026 :tutorial/title "Calculating Average Velocity Using Integration"} :step/id #uuid "12ea46a3-a7a9-5273-ae68-f843dba89bf3" :step/math-academy-id 2016 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097592}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097592 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096719 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096525 :question/id #uuid "62944da5-875f-542a-9b54-9c3d3c5db947" :question/math-academy-id "e-573"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "803ac4f0-61fe-5efc-b7a5-c73a4235f4bc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049280}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097294}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Average Velocity of a Particle Using Integration"} :step/id #uuid "7fa5d198-6f76-58fe-a0de-a2eede2c04f2" :step/math-academy-id 2017 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097676}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097593 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096720 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096526 :question/id #uuid "aa1e2c54-80dc-5e38-a149-c7db63deb418" :question/math-academy-id "e-574"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3046ebca-8a30-5228-a003-b8155cd1a62a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097287} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097292} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527967}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Average Position or Acceleration of a Particle Using Integration"} :step/id #uuid "63d935f4-c3b0-5692-aa00-a8d6bc7db985" :step/math-academy-id 2019} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097676 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097881 :tutorial/content "We can also calculate the average position $x_{\\textrm{avg}}$ and average acceleration $a_{\\textrm{avg}}$ of a particle over an interval $[t_1, t_2]$ using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx_{avg} = \\frac{1}{t_{2} - t_{1}}\\int_{t_{1}}^{t_{2}}x(t)dt; a_{avg} = \\frac{1}{t_{2} - t_{1}}\\int_{t_{1}}^{t_{2}}a(t)dt\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8e1cdc1f-de87-5b91-9ad0-f2bcab98be3f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5192 :tutorial/title "Calculating Average Position and Acceleration Using Integration"} :step/id #uuid "4f5e263b-c885-5894-bec5-5d0237b91689" :step/math-academy-id 32551 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097593}}] :activity/title "Average Position, Velocity, and Acceleration" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096868}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069637} :activity/id #uuid "a5eebdd0-457e-5351-b479-fdec034c955a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049227 :topic/id #uuid "e5039159-d776-5d2a-8b00-bb798bc20935" :topic/math-academy-id 446} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069637 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070036 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given the integral  $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{3x+2}{(x+1)^2} \\textrm{d}x.$ Note that we have the repeated factor $(x+1)$ in the denominator. \n\nThe correct form of the partial fraction expansion of the integrand is\n \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3x+2}{(x+1)^2}=\\dfrac{A}{(x+1)} +\\dfrac{B}{(x+1)^2} \\,.\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A$ and $B,$ we first multiply both sides the above equation by $(x+1)^2$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2 & =A(x+1)+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-1,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(-1)+2 & =A(-1+1)+B \\\\ B & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $B,$ we can solve for $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(0)+2 & =A(0+1)+B \\\\ 2 & =A+B \\\\ 2 & =A-1 \\\\ A & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3x+2}{(x+1)^2}= \\dfrac{3}{(x+1)} -\\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^2}.\n$$\n\nNow this function can be integrated term by term. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3x+2}{(x+1)^{2}}\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{3}{(x+1)}-\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}|x+1|+\\frac{1}{x+1}+C\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant of integration." :tutorial/id #uuid "de271889-71f4-5fa2-8346-7ed8fa03e000" :tutorial/math-academy-id 922 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d3ab3699-46bb-5348-b28d-d90ed53084d6" :step/math-academy-id 1800 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069644}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069644 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068743 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068587 :question/id #uuid "2fb19e4b-c455-599f-8856-5bee85f8efc4" :question/math-academy-id "e-508"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "05dd17d8-4170-5392-89e7-7547184b8556" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050964}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069284} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069384} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319133} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319251} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319253} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332784}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Rational Functions With Repeated Factors of Degree Two"} :step/id #uuid "a1f73cdf-ffed-50a9-b236-c48f569059a5" :step/math-academy-id 1801 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069724}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069648 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068747 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068588 :question/id #uuid "789c5233-a263-55a7-9b27-c834b2ceaf8d" :question/math-academy-id "e-509"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11647cdd-6315-5718-9425-88f4fb93faa7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069285} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069531} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250438} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250454} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250455} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250456} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351461} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351462}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Rational Functions With a Repeated Factor of Degree Higher Than Two"} :step/id #uuid "fac6b988-f49b-56f2-aa72-c550c04fafb0" :step/math-academy-id 1805} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069723 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068799 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068642 :question/id #uuid "79049868-d542-5461-ab14-4a4336c9221d" :question/math-academy-id "e-7275"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf7f11d9-6aa8-5922-a5fa-89f7dba66644" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048375}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069530} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235358} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250431} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319137} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319255} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323751} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323756}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Rational Functions With Repeated Factors of Degree Two That Require Factoring"} :step/id #uuid "e2a84f61-3405-5b4c-a923-cf909df72a37" :step/math-academy-id 26940 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069648}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069724 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068800 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068643 :question/id #uuid "d9f5cd99-436c-5089-a699-b9f9ff245157" :question/math-academy-id "e-7298"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "68e983da-1f69-5be1-8034-9422a84ce6be" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247404} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319254} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323766} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527939}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Rational Functions With a Repeated Factor and Another Factor"} :step/id #uuid "08d4211a-dda1-5079-96e1-bb188234b652" :step/math-academy-id 27007 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069723}}] :activity/title "Integrating Rational Functions with Repeated Factors" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068974}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185006} :activity/id #uuid "af91633b-288c-52bd-a641-7172621608f6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049327 :topic/id #uuid "ec1d59b5-66a8-5507-9862-10567e3ce6ab" :topic/math-academy-id 3834} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184944 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184177 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184078 :question/id #uuid "7dbb258d-fe71-5e7b-9501-c88ffdb6fe86" :question/math-academy-id "e-629"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2216cdfc-7f2a-5c53-b10b-2d66f35fbf32" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050162} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050277}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184535} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184796} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527917}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Tangent Lines to Polar Curves"} :step/id #uuid "a0ead2f8-7726-5a22-ad4d-582c7b4b8968" :step/math-academy-id 2206 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185001}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185001 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184215 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184120 :question/id #uuid "59156f24-72a1-5642-ac66-f84002c87d0b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9955"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e95ede7-306b-55e1-9245-7493f0afb514" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184437} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184536}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Normal Lines to Polar Curves"} :step/id #uuid "e6c2d34a-6683-50c4-a82e-b71e985d4ed8" :step/math-academy-id 37487} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185006 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185342 :tutorial/content "![](images/a0/a0b4ad67ea80f6d6eec3a15b3df18d551d84263c7424405588bd15162ef4e7c2.png)\n\nThe polar curve shown above is defined as\n\n$$\nr(\\theta) = 4 - 3\\cos \\theta, \\qquad 0\\leq \\theta < 2\\pi\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nat the point $P$. The tangent to the curve at $P$ is shown in the diagram.\n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at $P$, we follow four steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nUsing techniques that we've previously discussed, we can show that\n\n$$\ny^{′}(θ) = 4\\cos θ - 3\\cos 2θ, x^{′}(θ) = 3\\sin 2θ - 4\\sin θ\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= (y^{′}(θ))/(x^{′}(θ)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4\\cos θ - 3\\cos 2θ}{3\\sin 2θ - 4\\sin θ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P$.\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{θ = π/2} \\\\\n&= (4\\cos (\\frac{π}{2}) - 3\\cos (2 \\cdot \\frac{π}{2}))/(3\\sin (2 \\cdot \\frac{π}{2}) - 4\\sin (\\frac{π}{2})) \\\\\n&= (4\\cos (\\frac{π}{2}) - 3\\cos (π))/(3\\sin (π) - 4\\sin (\\frac{π}{2})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{0 + 3}{0 - 4} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{3}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P$.\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= r\\cos θ \\\\\n&= (4 - 3\\cos θ)\\cos θ \\\\\n&= (4 - 3\\cos (\\frac{π}{2}))\\cos (\\frac{π}{2}) \\\\\n&= 0 \\\\\ny_{1} &= r\\sin θ \\\\\n&= (4 - 3\\cos θ)\\sin θ \\\\\n&= (4 - 3\\cos (\\frac{π}{2}))\\sin (\\frac{π}{2}) \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are\n\n$$\n\\left(0, 4 \\right)\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the tangent.\n\nWe find the equation of the tangent as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - 4 &= -\\frac{3}{4} \\cdot (x - 0) \\\\\ny &= -\\frac{3}{4}x + 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "febc7b49-9662-5e78-a7a5-cbe9b1f596ba" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6186 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a1e51e4b-71cf-5749-aae1-c001b3a5a59b" :step/math-academy-id 37501 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184944}}] :activity/title "Tangent and Normal Lines to Polar Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186184419}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084199} :activity/id #uuid "6b249749-cdb1-5488-bab7-fd455ccb8cb2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049404 :topic/id #uuid "f1c81203-ccec-5577-930f-78f9306ef1d9" :topic/math-academy-id 691} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084039 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084428 :tutorial/content "We've been using the following formula for the sum to infinity of a geometric series with first term $a_1$ and common ratio $r$:\n\n$$\nS_\\infty = \\dfrac{a_1}{1-r}, \\qquad \\mid r \\mid < 1\n$$\n\nTo see where this formula comes from, first, we remember the formula for the sum of the first $n$ terms of a geometric series is given by\n\n$$\nS_n = a_1 \\cdot \\dfrac {1 - r ^ n} {1 - r}\n$$\n\nNotice that if $\\mid r \\mid < 1$ then $r^n\\to 0$ as $n\\to\\infty$. Therefore, as $n\\to\\infty$, we have:\n\n$$\nS_{n} \\mid → a_{1} \\cdot \\frac{1 - 0}{1 - r}; = a_{1} \\cdot \\frac{1}{1 - r}; = \\frac{a_{1}}{1 - r}; = S_{∞}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "663988c8-25f5-59d4-acf5-f842aa16de74" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2833 :tutorial/title "Justification of the Formula"} :step/id #uuid "62505009-7a0f-51cc-9a7b-25de0940cb7c" :step/math-academy-id 15070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084199 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084395 :tutorial/content "The sum $S_\\infty$ of an infinite geometric series with first term $a_1$ and common ratio $r$ can be computed using the formula\n\n$$\nS_\\infty = \\dfrac{a_1}{1-r}, \\qquad \\mid r \\mid < 1\n$$\n\nNote that $\\mid r \\mid < 1$ is a very important condition for the sum to exist. If the condition $\\mid r \\mid <1$ is *not* satisfied, then the formula does *not* work!\n\nFor example, suppose we want to calculate the sum of the infinite geometric series\n\n$$\n3 + 1 + \\dfrac 1 3 + \\dfrac 1 9 + \\dfrac{1}{27} + \\cdots\\,\n$$\n\nThe first term is $a_1=3$, and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac{a_2}{a_1} = \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have $\\mid r \\mid <1$. Therefore, the sum is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{∞} &= \\frac{a_{1}}{1 - r} \\\\\n&= (3)/((1 - \\frac{1}{3})) \\\\\n&= (3)/((\\frac{2}{3})) \\\\\n&= 3(\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{9}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n3 + 1 + \\dfrac 1 3 + \\dfrac 1 9 + \\dfrac{1}{27} + \\cdots = \\dfrac{9}{2}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "934f437a-4324-59f0-8c7b-7b41b40ea25b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1193 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1c5667ee-1f09-5553-bc41-a03da6922e4a" :step/math-academy-id 2341 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084200}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084200 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083277 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083081 :question/id #uuid "272c7490-d3b2-557b-8b23-4d24e4208dfe" :question/math-academy-id "e-679"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55b978ec-9ec6-5349-a103-0c11d9a904c1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050807} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050977}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083474} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083530} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233444} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234521}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Sum to Infinity of a Geometric Series Expressed Using Fractions"} :step/id #uuid "a33759e6-8395-5d82-80e7-58e9213ebee6" :step/math-academy-id 2342 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084202}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084202 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083279 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083082 :question/id #uuid "5eff5a85-f47d-5d1b-84e7-011a61ff8fb2" :question/math-academy-id "e-680"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2954b59f-d701-502f-963b-b62098a87c34" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083531} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233019} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319200} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321068} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345110}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Sum to Infinity of a Geometric Series Expressed Using Decimals"} :step/id #uuid "8378cca3-a8c1-5927-be8f-f71abbd197d0" :step/math-academy-id 2345 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084204}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084204 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083281 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083083 :question/id #uuid "552467cb-c641-5b17-802a-c9cf26d95c1e" :question/math-academy-id "e-681"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0adffc7-ef35-5f7a-a790-06addb354c2e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049083}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083907} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083908} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233996} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234027} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234228} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234230} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234231} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259691} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326814} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326827}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Sum to Infinity of a Geometric Series Given Two of Its Terms"} :step/id #uuid "212cc847-3bff-5269-b811-32a7904c769a" :step/math-academy-id 2348 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084039}}] :activity/title "Finding the Sum of an Infinite Geometric Series" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186083403}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097751} :activity/id #uuid "f055ed71-ae5b-569a-8cbe-acd4850a5cce" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049507 :topic/id #uuid "fa777873-dae5-503a-8b8d-d333b158b938" :topic/math-academy-id 1080} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097751 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097803 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to *approximate*\n\n$$\nf(x) = e^x\n$$\n\nfor some value of $x$. We can do this using the second-degree Taylor polynomial about a point $x=a$. For example, the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $e^x$ about the point $x=1$ is shown below:\n\n$$\ne^x \\approx P_2(x) = e + e(x-1) + \\dfrac 1 2 e(x-1)^2\n$$\n\nIn general, the polynomial $P_2(x)$ is only an approximation to the function $f(x)$, which means that there will usually be some error whenever we use $P_2$ to approximate $f$. We want to know how bad this error can get. Can we find an upper bound for the error?\n\nLet $R_2(x)$ be the **error** (or **remainder**) that we get when we use $P_2$ to approximate\n\n$$\nf(x)=e^x\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\nR_2(x) = e^x - P_2(x)\n$$\n\nThe **Lagrange error bound** states that an upper bound for $R_2$ is given by,\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}R_2 & \\leq \\dfrac{M}{3!}\\end{vmatrix}x-\\left.a\\right|^{3}\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ the maximum value of $\\mid f^{(3)}(x) \\mid$ on the interval between $a$ and $x$.\n\nIn our case, since our Taylor polynomial is taken about the point $a=1$, the Lagrange error bound becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}R_2 & \\leq \\dfrac{M}{3!}\\end{vmatrix}x-\\left.1\\right|^{3}\n$$\n\nNotice that the Lagrange error bound looks a lot like the last term of the *next* Taylor polynomial, $P_3(x)$.\n\nThe tricky part is finding the value of $M$. We'll learn how to do that in the following example." :tutorial/id #uuid "fcb4053a-c61a-538b-8b44-5a92f73f053f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1292 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c93a74b3-e513-5fb9-9595-6bf177ec940a" :step/math-academy-id 4844 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097752}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097752 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096820 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096562 :question/id #uuid "c946c1c9-f3d2-5d9d-9390-8ee542ca15a5" :question/math-academy-id "e-745"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "360b8076-1b27-5630-a07e-221a0e53011d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048515} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050866} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050955}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097317}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Upper Bound for the Error Using the Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial"} :step/id #uuid "82542d7a-3ec1-5979-8dd2-13a732ad5eb1" :step/math-academy-id 4845 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097753}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097753 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097804 :tutorial/content "The Lagrange error bound that we've discussed so far is valid only for the error associated with a second-degree Taylor polynomial. But we can generalize the result to higher-order Taylor polynomials.\n\nLet $R_n(x)$ be the error involved in approximating a function $f(x)$ by its $n$th-degree Taylor polynomial $P_n(x)$ about $x=a$. So, we have\n\n$$\nR_n(x) = f(x) - P_n(x)\n$$\n\nThen the Lagrange error bound for $R_n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}R_n(x) & \\leq \\dfrac{M}{(n+1)!}\\end{vmatrix}x-\\left.a\\right|^{n+1}\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $\\mid f^{(n+1)} \\mid$ on the interval between $a$ and $x$." :tutorial/id #uuid "07b10e6a-1673-5d6e-8572-8b4505579f44" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1294 :tutorial/title "The General Result of the Lagrange Error Bound"} :step/id #uuid "13415159-b315-5389-bc81-61ef044820e2" :step/math-academy-id 4849 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097754}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097754 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096821 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096563 :question/id #uuid "3f5e5945-cdaa-55b9-b360-9e68249480b7" :question/math-academy-id "e-746"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "79a10073-8798-597e-821c-691b0dad5ae7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096961} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527920}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Upper Bound for the Error Using the Third-Degree Taylor Polynomial"} :step/id #uuid "b70fcb39-7efc-50ad-a28c-73509579c61d" :step/math-academy-id 4850}] :activity/title "The Lagrange Error Bound" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096869}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1380.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079388} :activity/id #uuid "90d79c17-70ff-5e9b-8f6f-cc71f6ec671c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049650 :topic/id #uuid "07fde130-1b24-5086-ac8d-0443c3af56b8" :topic/math-academy-id 648} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079388 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079607 :tutorial/content "For a particle moving in the $xy$-plane with position vector $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ we calculate the velocity vector $\\mathbf{v}(t)$ by differentiating $\\mathbf{r}$ with respect to $t.$ So, how do we find the position vector $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ of a particle given its velocity $\\mathbf{v}(t)?$\n\nWe need to do the reverse of differentiation, so we integrate $\\mathbf{v}(t)$ with respect to $t\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}(t) = \\frac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r}}{\\textrm{d}t} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\mathbf{r}(t) = \\int \\mathbf{v}(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe're often given the position at some time $t,$ and we can determine the arbitrary constant of integration using that information." :tutorial/id #uuid "86fda900-3920-531f-aae7-34d7141e5eea" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1115 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3bc53c85-8fb5-53fa-8831-b99bb3039a65" :step/math-academy-id 2193 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079389}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079389 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078483 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078331 :question/id #uuid "14f41e91-3d92-5977-b42f-578d4caba951" :question/math-academy-id "e-625"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "47989094-35d2-561f-add4-f1d427cd8a20" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050951}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256638} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320791} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332876} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403537}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Position Vector Given a Velocity Vector"} :step/id #uuid "0d98d580-624f-5dab-997b-63fb52fe39c5" :step/math-academy-id 2194 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079390}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079390 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078484 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078332 :question/id #uuid "b5a4461e-fda6-50d2-98df-0c4937cc3e50" :question/math-academy-id "e-626"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ff12d64-7e03-596b-83e6-c91d22600452" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079052} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320792} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320793} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351510} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354415} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387187}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Position Vector Given a Velocity Vector and Time"} :step/id #uuid "f6dd2a26-a41b-5180-8053-8f7f3b27d963" :step/math-academy-id 2197 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079391}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079391 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078485 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078333 :question/id #uuid "ea193f94-9171-5f20-9167-207761800c93" :question/math-academy-id "e-627"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49aa6b33-42fb-5f28-89c5-019eadcaf1a0" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079051} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079211} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319169} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320804} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320808} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320809} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354416}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Times When a Particle Is at a Certain Location Given Its Velocity Vector"} :step/id #uuid "1fc76cfc-b7b4-5a46-aac3-978342efa3ff" :step/math-academy-id 2200 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079414}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079414 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078504 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078356 :question/id #uuid "7ef2b355-cfca-5d57-b0f2-acd534663662" :question/math-academy-id "e-7343"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "279c5e4e-db19-584b-becb-10864fcba188" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050510}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320861} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320866} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339068} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387194} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527956}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Position Vector Given an Acceleration Vector"} :step/id #uuid "69c7797b-d04e-501d-a037-f445a1d28b90" :step/math-academy-id 27228}] :activity/title "Finding Displacement Vectors in Two Dimensions Using Integration" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078670}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101729} :activity/id #uuid "049d7046-a6c1-5a65-9468-c74225bebee4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049664 :topic/id #uuid "0927f000-da59-5e87-ac90-440a54290279" :topic/math-academy-id 1248} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101729 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101916 :tutorial/content "Suppose that the base of a solid is the semicircle enclosed by the curve\n\n$$\ny=-\\sqrt{4-x^2}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis, as shown in the picture below. Cross-sections perpendicular to the $x$-axis are rectangles. If the height of each rectangle is half of the length of its base, then what is the volume of the solid?\n\n![](images/60/605b9bfd34ba7035ab9533679f35a76f72e2d10b78f75d02ff7b351f4e330957.png)\n\nA sketch with a few cross-sections is shown in the picture below.\n\n![](images/de/de3426f274dd0a779c9721b257c80b8482b2f13e42b2433569194520431facee.png)\n\nIf we are given the expression $A(x)$ for the area of each cross-section, then we find the volume as follows:\n\n$$\nV = \\int_{a}^{b} A(x)\\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nConsider now a cross-section at some point $x$, where $x \\in (-2,2)$:\n\n![](images/b1/b14743ae0bbd16c7896762579397e766021c370f391d670d9bd6edbf8630c297.png)\n\nIf we project the rectangular cross-section onto the $xy$-plane, we get the following picture:\n\n![](images/b7/b78d390bd7ce359377716c9ed757c52131cb9e0609f68b1c3b1d31d8368977c3.png)\n\nThen the base of the cross-section is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb &= \\mid y \\mid \\\\\n&= \\mid - \\sqrt{4 - x^{2}} \\mid \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4 - x^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the height ($h$) of each rectangle is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\ntimes the base ($b$), the area of the rectangle is\n\n$$\nA = bh = b \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2} b \\right) = \\dfrac{1}{2}b^2\n$$\n\nSo, the expression for the area of the cross-section is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA(x) &= \\frac{1}{2}b^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{4 - x^{2}})^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(4 - x^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now calculate the volume of the solid using the following integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= \\int_{a}^{b}A(x)dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{-2}^{2}(4 - x^{2})dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "98422b67-38f6-5ab8-855d-49dab00c658b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1035 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0d6b036f-4e13-5543-8030-db3a557fefa6" :step/math-academy-id 2033 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101730}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101730 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100901 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100738 :question/id #uuid "a7e161d5-edb5-5adb-ab72-2edad67b0a9c" :question/math-academy-id "e-579"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2e18711-6114-5c11-ad18-7b06bc0278d8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049608} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050272}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101460}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume When the Base Is Bounded by a Curve"} :step/id #uuid "012b20cf-a9ef-5688-858c-bc9067e86f98" :step/math-academy-id 2034 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101775}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101731 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101917 :tutorial/content "Let's now suppose that the base of a solid is the right triangle enclosed by the line\n\n$$\nx=2y+2\n$$\n\nand the $y$-axis between $y=-1$ and $y=0$, as shown in the picture below.\n\n![](images/8c/8cb3b29bfc46768c487e00416298829011302cb3d2641e80c7f9ca56466c44ac.png)\n\nSuppose further that cross-sections perpendicular to the $\\mathbf y$**-axis** are rectangles and that the height of each rectangle is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\ntimes the length of its base. How do we find the volume of that solid?\n\nA sketch with a few cross-sections is shown in the picture below.\n\n![](images/fc/fc2555c55658aa50b57da6f07a41bbc276eba7ac07ae42cd83fd078f20126cbf.png)\n\nIf we are given the expression $A(y)$ for the area of each cross-section, then we find the volume as follows:\n\n$$\nV = \\int_{c}^{d} A(y)\\:\\textrm{d}y\n$$\n\nConsider now a cross-section at some point $y$, where $y \\in (-1,0)$:\n\n![](images/29/292852165b43623bd36923d13244f08f0d9c87a0b97b5b8437eb1ac3a9cee39a.png)\n\nBy projecting the rectangular cross-section onto the $xy$-plane, we get the following picture:\n\n![](images/4e/4ef6cd638cac080303e1d62f6a5e82a63cd03793feafc59898e10da462abcc1b.png)\n\nThen, the base of the cross-section is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb &= \\begin{vmatrix}x|= & 2y + 2\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the height $h$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\ntimes the base $b$, the area of the rectangle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA(x) &= bh \\\\\n&= b \\cdot (\\frac{3}{4}b) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{4}b^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{4}(2y + 2)^{2} \\\\\n&= 3(y + 1)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now calculate the volume of the solid using the following integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= \\int_{c}^{d}A(y)dy \\\\\n&= 3\\int_{-1}^{0}(y + 1)^{2}dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6a961f5d-0ae1-54f8-bd71-bc7abf04c657" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1037 :tutorial/title "Cross-Sections Perpendicular to the y-Axis"} :step/id #uuid "58505320-d804-56b2-b1d1-e26e0e56c0a9" :step/math-academy-id 2037 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101736}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101732 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100902 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100739 :question/id #uuid "327cf409-45b7-5ca0-9b6e-80dcd04e2cae" :question/math-academy-id "e-580"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d02653d8-cc5c-5742-ac03-b19c4e2e81a8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050548}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101462}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume When the Base Is Bounded by Two Curves"} :step/id #uuid "fe6b488e-9033-5b93-8ecd-cc59251d25d9" :step/math-academy-id 2038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101736 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100905 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100740 :question/id #uuid "c012e43c-0634-560d-a95b-03ae55c20809" :question/math-academy-id "e-581"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "92643fc8-d1ce-5ec4-b44b-abf362dd2357" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050393}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101296} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101461}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume  When the Cross Sections Are Perpendicular to the y-Axis"} :step/id #uuid "6fdcba45-8f89-5715-929d-c9a7335cacf9" :step/math-academy-id 2041 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101732}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101775 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100932 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100764 :question/id #uuid "cd1bed05-184b-59ba-bfb8-ed49f0d5122c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6823"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8cae38a9-2bca-52d7-8948-d526a235eadf" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049042}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101524} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527970}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume When the Base Is Bounded by Two Curves"} :step/id #uuid "abeb624a-f236-52b4-b78b-940eb2a4cbc2" :step/math-academy-id 25319 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101731}}] :activity/title "Volumes of Solids with Rectangular Cross Sections" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186101088}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189104} :activity/id #uuid "cd68d011-8836-5efd-ab3b-ba09c700a3e9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049739 :topic/id #uuid "0fe4fee1-477c-5881-86c7-1921fd5fff4e" :topic/math-academy-id 3860} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189054 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188257 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188203 :question/id #uuid "012abf23-a2df-5479-a38f-3c76c099a39f" :question/math-academy-id "e-524"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d868c89-ca9e-589d-917e-cfec472a44f6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050288} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050411}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188542} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188543} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373820} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373827} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373834} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373841} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501189} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501193}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Two-Dimensional Related Rates Using Differential Equations"} :step/id #uuid "da66f4c9-69eb-512a-b43d-9289b5f8fb5a" :step/math-academy-id 1853 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189107}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189104 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189375 :tutorial/content "When constructing a differential equation to model a particular phenomenon, we sometimes combine several pieces of information to create the correct model.\n\nConsider the following situation:\n\n*The common cold virus spreads through a particular country. The rate at which the number of infected people increases is **proportional** to the **square of the time** $t$ (in days) since the outbreak started. \nAdditionally, $100$ infected people **recover from the virus each day**.*\n\nWe want to find a differential equation that models $P(t),$ the number of infected people. We note the following:\n\n- The statement describes the *rate* at which the *number of infected people* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d P}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\n- The rate at which the number of infected people increases is **proportional** to the **square of the time** $t.$ Therefore, if $I(t)$ represents the rate at which infections increase, then\n\n$$\nI(t) = kt^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a positive constant.\n\n- Additionally, $100$ infected people **recover from the virus each day**. Therefore, if $D(t)$ represents the rate at which infections decrease, then\n\n$$\nD(t) = 100.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the differential equation that describes this phenomenon is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t} & ={{k{t}^{2}-100}_{⏟}}_{I(t)-D(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlthough we introduced the functions $I(t)$ and $D(t)$ here, we can usually write down the appropriate differential equation straight away. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "8a7ad3b5-e4de-56c0-8fc9-8a9b2c976094" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6270 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "37206e3d-9800-567e-82f6-eed74a977f1f" :step/math-academy-id 38001 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189105}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189105 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188290 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186187994 :question/id #uuid "67d01a20-4e4b-5b03-a2a3-f4b21cd81662" :question/math-academy-id "e-10072"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9609247-0e03-57ea-b5ae-e4d8317f21cd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048746} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048835}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188913} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373715} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375082} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375089} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501160}] :knowledge-point/title "Translating Contextual Problems Into Differential Equations"} :step/id #uuid "9da430dc-8eca-5286-8278-c764eba32b5d" :step/math-academy-id 38002 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189245}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189107 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188291 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186187995 :question/id #uuid "1b0483b6-a42d-51fb-9ccd-fe6cb2a7acc1" :question/math-academy-id "e-10075"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba211dba-23ad-5755-b1e3-082e13be59a2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048542}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188912} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373708} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373799} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373806} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501204}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Three-Dimensional Related Rates Using Differential Equations"} :step/id #uuid "53525e67-0e77-588f-88a9-1bda7f5964de" :step/math-academy-id 38012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189108 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188292 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186187996 :question/id #uuid "b657adbb-c3c4-54c2-8c2c-44ccc0bf9c3a" :question/math-academy-id "e-10076"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "474f9c82-4ae7-58e0-9457-6bda9cab8973" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049688} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050975}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188915} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188916} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373792} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373855} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373862} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373869} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373876} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501219}] :knowledge-point/title "Translating Coordinate Plane Problems Into Differential Equations"} :step/id #uuid "ccce909c-08ed-53ce-a30b-e357e8de3d59" :step/math-academy-id 38016 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189054}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189245 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189439 :tutorial/content "Let's recall some basic facts from geometry in the $xy$-plane:\n\n- The distance between the point $(x,y)$ and the origin $(0,0)$ equals $\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}.$\n\n- The (smallest) distance between the point $(x,y)$ and the $x$-axis equals $|y|.$\n\n- Similarly, the (smallest) distance between the point $(x,y)$ and the $y$-axis equals $|x|.$\n\n- The slope of the line that passes through $(x,y)$ and the origin equals $\\dfrac{y}{x}.$\n\nWe sometimes need to use these basic facts when constructing differential equations described in a geometric context. \n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "b03d6daa-af90-5f14-bf0f-929da8526673" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8152 :tutorial/title "Recalling Some Facts From Elementary Geometry"} :step/id #uuid "d499c1d3-7173-5509-b128-c42561cce342" :step/math-academy-id 53348 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189108}}] :activity/title "Further Modeling With First-Order ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186188455}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097608} :activity/id #uuid "ccadebeb-ab4f-5e8c-b59d-112db894f229" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049885 :topic/id #uuid "1b27fe0a-58ff-57f2-8e77-9341dd7fc153" :topic/math-academy-id 1082} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097608 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097796 :tutorial/content "The region enclosed by the curves $y=x^4$ and $y=x$ is shown below. Suppose that this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the horizontal line $y=-1$, generating a solid of revolution. How can we write an integral expression that gives the volume of the solid?\n\n![](images/18/1880138e4a20cb5a47ba89cb3177d5dc6e74f998d7fdd428cdded7af38cadd02.png)\n\nFirst, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{4} &= x \\\\\nx^{4} - x &= 0 \\\\\nx(x^{3} - 1) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 0, 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=1$.\n\nNow, let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/49/49da0885bbf23e586b488e2b2a9bcaa0d923d3591833c9b8beb31a611c1d8b43.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y=x^4$, and\n- *leaves* though the outer curve $y=x$.\n\nNow, we can express the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves, as follows:\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y=x$.![](images/35/352ff26bb5dce0a923c102c4528bd89555b49203c212fb73a42cf31cdf8b226e.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_{outer} &= π\\int_{0}^{1}[(upper) - (lower)]^{2}dx \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{1}[(x) - (-1)]^{2}dx \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{1}[x + 1]^{2}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y=x^4$.![](images/ee/ee0fa31b61acd4a1f2a9b6bb21dcd0e5eed3c529780fcb67096d40dccf90c20c.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_{inner} &= π\\int_{0}^{1}[(upper) - (lower)]^{2}dx \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{1}[(x^{4}) - (-1)]^{2}dx \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{1}[x^{4} + 1]^{2}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n$V|= V_{outer} - V_{inner}; = π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}[x + 1]^{2}dx - π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}[x^{4} + 1]^{2}dx; = π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}([x + 1]^{2} - [x^{4} + 1]^{2})dx$." :tutorial/id #uuid "8e33de60-9df4-540e-b19b-f12088b349e2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1064 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "626bee34-cf24-50f9-b6b3-1fcbce8fe1a3" :step/math-academy-id 2092 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097659}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097609 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096729 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096531 :question/id #uuid "b6dd2970-3cdc-58fc-8f28-0bfdcd738d6f" :question/math-academy-id "e-593"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2e0268f-36a5-5f55-a0ae-9e0f34981dab" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097228} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097229}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Horizontal Line"} :step/id #uuid "52be5c09-e883-51da-a981-64e4b7986356" :step/math-academy-id 2093 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097610}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097610 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097797 :tutorial/content "Now, suppose that the region enclosed by the curves\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac x2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt x\n$$\n\nis rotated $360^\\circ$ about the vertical line $x=-2$. Let's find the volume of the resulting solid.\n\n![](images/78/7876211e2781a079ac2444436e671e2d61c28ec09774c0acad65e96fb537927a.png)\n\nFirst, since we are rotating about a vertical line, we need to solve the equations of our curves for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}y = \\frac{x}{2} & ⟹x = 2y \\\\ y = \\sqrt{x} & ⟹x = y^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2y &= y^{2} \\\\\ny^{2} - 2y &= 0 \\\\\ny(y - 2) &= 0 \\\\\ny &= 0, 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y={2}$.\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/c3/c3fd84335240208fb158da9b67aaffa07cf490c67f8a77bf28947df998180b76.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x={y^2}$, and\n- *leaves* though the outer curve $x=2y$.\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=2y$.![](images/6a/6a075f9b5fb64923de7037b31ec6dd0408e88aa6d965ebb8b204b6c560e23ec1.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_{outer} &= π\\int_{0}^{2}[(right) - (left)]^{2}dy \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{2}[(2y) - (-2)]^{2}dy \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{2}[2y + 2]^{2}dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=y^2$.![](images/cc/cca4c498956a16cb374a8d0b3341a09e52844026593557710525d07be79a233a.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_{inner} &= π\\int_{0}^{2}[(right) - (left)]^{2}dy \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{2}[(y^{2}) - (-2)]^{2}dy \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{2}[y^{2} + 2]^{2}dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.Finally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n$V|= V_{outer} - V_{inner}; = π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}[2y + 2]^{2}dy - π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}[y^{2} + 2]^{2}dy; = π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}([2y + 2]^{2} - [y^{2} + 2]^{2})dy; = π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}(-y^{4} + 8y)dy; = \\left.π(-\\frac{y^{5}}{5} + 4y^{2})\\right|_{0}^{2}; = π[(-\\frac{32}{5} + 16) - 0]; = \\frac{48π}{5}$." :tutorial/id #uuid "78a4b0ee-944d-5de3-846a-bf591d05387b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1066 :tutorial/title "Rotation About a Vertical Line"} :step/id #uuid "ca0b12a1-0e89-5062-b87e-358443f928e2" :step/math-academy-id 2097 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097660}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097611 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096730 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096532 :question/id #uuid "b79b0d88-0b42-5223-927b-5a4023f6c329" :question/math-academy-id "e-594"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c6aa618-4d2f-5ce2-9bb2-c6b528ec9b7b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097209}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Vertical Line"} :step/id #uuid "bb053558-c7a0-533b-bcf7-e8427cfa707a" :step/math-academy-id 2098} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097659 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096764 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096569 :question/id #uuid "8e436e4c-45da-528c-885b-9a094919a652" :question/math-academy-id "e-7508"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9016e61-3365-5f53-ae10-cc94e9c638f3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048539} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048643}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097206} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097207}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Horizontal Line"} :step/id #uuid "38143ed9-ca20-5c45-b750-274568e87032" :step/math-academy-id 27864 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097609}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097660 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096765 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096570 :question/id #uuid "d064d066-3bbe-5381-ab56-3fc407da144f" :question/math-academy-id "e-7509"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26659894-bc6d-50fe-a5f8-029c962452cc" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097212} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097307} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527958}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Vertical Line"} :step/id #uuid "3a272f90-abf1-569a-853d-f9c7cfbd0534" :step/math-academy-id 27865 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097611}}] :activity/title "Volumes of Revolution Using the Washer Method: Rotation About Other Axes" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096871}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065096} :activity/id #uuid "22d1805e-71b9-5243-96d1-5454b5d68ac6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050299 :topic/id #uuid "3d9b9134-f2c7-559d-b631-25a1da8ede8d" :topic/math-academy-id 338} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065096 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065438 :tutorial/content "How do we calculate $\\displaystyle \\int\\dfrac{3x+2}{x+2}\\,\\textrm d x\\,?$\n\nWe need to write the integrand so that it's something we can integrate. One way to do this is to divide $3x+2$ by $x+2$ using polynomial or synthetic division. We'll use synthetic division because it's faster. \n\nTo rewrite $\\dfrac{3x+2}{x+2},$ we complete a synthetic division table.\n\n|  | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}-2$ | $3$ | $2$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $3$ | $-4$ |\n\nThis tells us that $\\dfrac{3x+2}{x+2}$ can be decomposed into a quotient and remainder as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3x+2}{x+2}= 3 - \\dfrac{4}{x+2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can integrate the function, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3x+2}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (3-\\frac{4}{x+2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 3\\,\\text{d}x-4\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3x-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2437a8a0-d1de-5b85-8577-7e7493c4d520" :tutorial/math-academy-id 884 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "83b3796b-b6b4-589d-a5b3-e83dd146da2d" :step/math-academy-id 630729 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065102}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065102 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064235 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064096 :question/id #uuid "4d4edfc8-e178-58b4-b827-e4310ead130e" :question/math-academy-id "e-485"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "236f51c2-22ad-551f-afb4-62fd6de6d21e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051061}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064863} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064864} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320961} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320962} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332703} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332704} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332710} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332711} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332712} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489203} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489259} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489293}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Quotient of Linear Polynomials"} :step/id #uuid "4eb00783-0e9f-5f56-a7c2-2671083182b4" :step/math-academy-id 630730 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065103}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065103 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064236 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064097 :question/id #uuid "74761123-d539-5d78-8dcd-4ea1db04bed0" :question/math-academy-id "e-486"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f110daa3-fd31-5e34-8a88-0e002c2e418a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064865} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064866} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250311} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319178} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320966} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320967} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320968} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332713} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332714} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332722} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332723} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332724} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380643} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489231} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489288}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Rational Function Whose Denominator is Linear"} :step/id #uuid "8a270b04-626b-5faf-b8e7-a60d62b9d521" :step/math-academy-id 630733 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065113}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065113 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064244 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064098 :question/id #uuid "cf016fa7-c3a2-5f9b-84a6-7b005d0d7286" :question/math-academy-id "e-487"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7d71bc2-c0f0-5504-9f79-a5f5cdb8cd92" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064867} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064868} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250318} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323727} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323728} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332705} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332725} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332726} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341960} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341961} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341962} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489256} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489260} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489275}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Rational Function Whose Denominator Contains a Leading Coefficient"} :step/id #uuid "5f0e5f61-e451-55c5-a8ca-ae1e6b3c2676" :step/math-academy-id 630736}] :activity/title "Integrating Functions Using Polynomial Division" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064429}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180353} :activity/id #uuid "0e333d7b-9586-5dcc-a2e9-29e8919f1fde" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050442 :topic/id #uuid "49568fcd-874d-513a-8a2c-8089246092fb" :topic/math-academy-id 3674} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180353 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180792 :tutorial/content "Recall that a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt} = ky(a-y)\n$$\n\nis called a logistic growth equation. The positive constant $a > 0$ is the carrying capacity, and $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nA typical solution curve $y = y(t)$ to this equation is shown below. \n\n![](images/0f/0f416479bbf72d3b9c1bf48861883e0e77bbc1c1d60d7fa48fb89e6f21c104eb.png)\n\nIt can be shown that the general solution to this equation is given by\n\n$$\ny(t) = \\frac{a}{1+C e^{-ak t}}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integration constant. \n\nNote the following properties of this general solution:\n\n- Since $a$ and $k$ are both positive, $e^{-akt}\\to0$ as $t\\to\\infty.$ Therefore, $y(t)\\to a$ as $t\\to\\infty.$ In other words, $y(t)$ approaches the carrying capacity as $t$ increases. This agrees with the behavior shown in our diagram.\n\n- Since $e^{-akt}\\to\\infty$ as $t\\to-\\infty,$ we have that $y(t)\\to 0$ as $t\\to-\\infty.$ This also agrees with our diagram.\n\nWe can use the general solution of the logistic growth equation to make predictions about a population governed by logistic growth. Let's see how." :tutorial/id #uuid "7ba42b62-b5a3-5c54-8a92-9ce67945fd36" :tutorial/math-academy-id 981 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3acea89b-a4db-5284-92df-1f9665ae536c" :step/math-academy-id 1924 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180397}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180396 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179676 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179532 :question/id #uuid "77bb1db2-e2fb-5126-a116-fce07c309d0d" :question/math-academy-id "e-7407"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "18af80e5-cd0f-5a1d-b299-2956fd886c5d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180288} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180289} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319205} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321098} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321099} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321100} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354262} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354450} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394532}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Particular Solution of a Logistic Growth Initial Value Problem to Make Predictions"} :step/id #uuid "a534edd7-0a0d-55a8-a499-ecfc5a548c59" :step/math-academy-id 27476 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180398}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180397 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179677 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179533 :question/id #uuid "e53b71f8-4ea8-50e7-81c6-62fbd2ac54ba" :question/math-academy-id "e-7408"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e71ba74-f952-56ef-885c-a32c4a4aec20" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048127} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048578}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253614} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253615} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321095} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321096} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321097} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354260} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354292} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354449} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528009}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Logistic Growth Initial Value Problem"} :step/id #uuid "b4f05448-8bec-515f-b01f-973c815d0ffd" :step/math-academy-id 27483 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180396}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180398 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180682 :tutorial/content "The general solution to the logistic growth equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = ky(a-y)\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\ny(t) = \\dfrac{a}{1+Ce^{-akt}}\n$$\n\nwhere $C>0$ is an integration constant. Let's now derive this formula.\n\nOur differential equation is separable, so we start by separating variables and then integrating with respect to $t\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dt} & =ky(a-y) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y(a-y)}\\frac{dy}{dt} & =k \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y(a-y)}\\frac{dy}{dt}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int k\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y(a-y)}\\,dy & =\\int k\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{ 1}{y(a-y)} = \\dfrac{1}{a}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{y} + \\dfrac{1}{a-y}\\right),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite the integrand on the left-hand side and complete the integration, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{y(a-y)}\\,dy & =\\int k\\,dt \\\\ \\frac{1}{a}\\int (\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{a-y})\\,dy & =\\int k\\,dt \\\\ \\int (\\frac{1}{y}-\\frac{1}{y-a})\\,dy & =ak\\int \\,dt \\\\ -\\int (\\frac{1}{y-a}-\\frac{1}{y})\\,dy & =ak\\int \\,dt \\\\ \\int (\\frac{1}{y-a}-\\frac{1}{y})\\,dy & =-ak\\int \\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}|y-a|-\\operatorname{ln}|y| & =-akt+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}|\\frac{y-a}{y}| & =-akt+{C}_{1} \\\\ |\\frac{y-a}{y}| & ={e}^{-akt+{C}_{1}} \\\\ |\\frac{y-a}{y}| & ={e}^{{C}_{1}}{e}^{-akt} \\\\ \\frac{y-a}{y} & =\\pm {e}^{{C}_{1}}{e}^{-akt} \\\\ \\frac{y-a}{y} & =\\pm C{e}^{-akt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=e^{C_1}$ is a positive constant. \n\nIn our modeling, we have assumed that $0 < y  < a.$ Therefore, the left-hand side of the equation must be negative since the numerator is negative and the denominator is positive. Therefore, we discard the positive option, and we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y-a}{y}  =  -C e^{-akt}.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-a}{y} & =-C{e}^{-akt} \\\\ y-a & =-Cy{e}^{-akt} \\\\ y+y\\cdot C{e}^{-akt} & =a \\\\ y(1+C{e}^{-akt}) & =a \\\\ y & =\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}\\,\\,\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ba0b74de-5c49-5d2e-a9a6-207220dbb90e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4521 :tutorial/title "Deriving the General Solution"} :step/id #uuid "8237a905-9644-5c44-a21f-8bcd1b0638a5" :step/math-academy-id 27816}] :activity/title "Solving the Logistic Growth Equation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179831}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084209} :activity/id #uuid "599eb266-615f-5404-b22e-4ae2f5fb387a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050529 :topic/id #uuid "508d3e6c-0cb1-59bd-b93a-329774355507" :topic/math-academy-id 689} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084209 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084397 :tutorial/content "A repeating decimal can be expressed as a geometric series whose common ratio is a power of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 {10}\n$$\n\nFor example, the number $0.\\overline{8}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.8^(―) &= 0.8 + 0.08 + 0.008 + … \\\\\n&= \\frac{8}{10} + \\frac{8}{100} + \\frac{8}{1000} + …\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\na_1 = \\dfrac{8}{10}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac {0.08}{0.8} = \\dfrac 1 {10}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.8^(―) &= ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{1}r^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{8}{10}(\\frac{1}{10})^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)8 \\cdot \\frac{1}{10} \\cdot (\\frac{1}{10})^{n} \\cdot (\\frac{1}{10})^{-1} \\\\\n&= ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)8 \\cdot \\frac{1}{10} \\cdot (\\frac{1}{10})^{n} \\cdot (\\frac{1}{10})^{-1} \\\\\n&= ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)8 \\cdot \\frac{1}{10} \\cdot (\\frac{1}{10})^{n} \\cdot 10 \\\\\n&= ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)8(\\frac{1}{10})^{n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3998b477-1c28-5edc-8605-2738ce474d94" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1212 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d0db37f3-c116-58a6-a33d-41ca478ae931" :step/math-academy-id 2380 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084210}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084210 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083284 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083086 :question/id #uuid "acebcd95-16ed-5087-8d04-0d6d503e31f3" :question/math-academy-id "e-694"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "367fe9fb-9d92-5105-9959-f3861994aa3c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050286}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083792} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083793}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Repeating Decimal as an Infinite Geometric Series"} :step/id #uuid "af145878-806f-54bc-991b-28bc9b80cc8a" :step/math-academy-id 2381 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084211}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084211 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083285 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083087 :question/id #uuid "ed24fd0c-69c1-502a-a05f-6e859724214a" :question/math-academy-id "e-695"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8813b8a0-3b5e-5cd8-a37d-6b921f2882b8" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083523} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083691} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234032}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Repeating Decimal as the Sum of a Number and a Geometric Series"} :step/id #uuid "4af12d7e-248c-5555-bd4c-98e9849f98a8" :step/math-academy-id 2385}] :activity/title "Repeating Decimals as Infinite Geometric Series" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186083401}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097612} :activity/id #uuid "71e9c379-9817-5da3-85f9-50847626fa5d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050787 :topic/id #uuid "67094ea1-3fa3-574a-a9fb-47317689906e" :topic/math-academy-id 1043} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097612 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097798 :tutorial/content "![](images/d8/d8f08b1cc474cfee367ccf52e2299deaeec146ef7a7a860f64a75f7f7f25cb0b.png)\n\nConsider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=t^3-1, \\qquad y=2t-t^3, \\qquad t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)\n$$\n\nThe shaded area shown in the diagram is the finite region bounded between the curve, the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines formed by $x$-axis and the points on the curve corresponding to $t_1=0$ to $t_2=1$, as shown.\n\nWe know that the area of the curve is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\int_{a}^{b} y \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nSince our curve is defined parametrically, we need to change the variable of integration from $x$ to $t$ using the change of variables formula, given by\n\n$$\nA = \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} y(t) \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm d t}\\:\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nNow, in this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt} = \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{3} - 1) = 3t^{2}\n$$\n\nApplying the change of variables formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\int_{t_{1}}^{t_{2}}y(t)\\frac{dx}{dt}dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}(2t - t^{3}) \\cdot 3t^{2}dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}6t^{3} - 3t^{5}dt \\\\\n&= 6\\int_{0}^{1}t^{3}dt - 3\\int_{0}^{1}t^{5}dt \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot \\left.\\frac{t^{4}}{4}\\right|_{0}^{1} - 3 \\cdot \\left.\\frac{t^{6}}{6}\\right|_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= 6(\\frac{1}{4} - 0) - 3(\\frac{1}{6} - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2} - \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shaded area shown in the picture has an area of $1$ square unit." :tutorial/id #uuid "1d0c890f-fbe0-5ded-948b-b94d13caac3b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1083 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "786032d8-d66a-579e-8336-348ca609cfb8" :step/math-academy-id 2136 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097747}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097613 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096731 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096538 :question/id #uuid "85879186-0866-5159-905b-65d043681231" :question/math-academy-id "e-604"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f354f758-3745-5cee-9a4b-a0878c2c8578" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050929}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096926} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097235} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527964}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area Bounded by a Parametric Curve"} :step/id #uuid "296a64b2-a271-5186-bf37-e47d7b56624c" :step/math-academy-id 2138} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097653 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096758 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096561 :question/id #uuid "95cdc31d-c3f2-50b6-9447-b7317af775f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-7412"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f20da72e-5a93-5a4e-be39-d412bd8fee86" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050531}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097133} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097134}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression for the Area Bounded by a Parametric Curve"} :step/id #uuid "3a032caf-6330-5e24-bafb-d45c348ad375" :step/math-academy-id 27495 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097613}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097747 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097915 :tutorial/content "In general, the area between the $x$-axis and the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx = x(t), \\qquad y = y(t), \\qquad t_1 \\leq t \\leq t_2\n$$\n\nis given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n\\int_{t_1}^{t_2} y \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} \\:\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When applying this formula, we assume that the chosen parametrization traverses the curve only once.\n- It's possible that this integral gives a negative value for the area even when the corresponding region lies entirely above the $x$-axis! To see why, note that if the curve lies above the $x$-axis, we must have $y(t) > 0$. However, if the curve is parameterized so that it is traversed from right to left, then $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} < 0$, and we have\n$y\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} < 0$\nand thus, our integral turns out to be negative.\n- To ensure that we get an unsigned area, we take the absolute value of the integral.\n- In general, care must be taken when asked to compute the area bounded by a parametric curve when $y\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}$ changes sign over the domain of integration. However, we won't consider that scenario here.\n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "89e96b74-e406-52f5-b64e-46267df927e4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7915 :tutorial/title "A General Formula for the Area Bounded by a Parametric Curve"} :step/id #uuid "97757635-74ae-5269-ac75-2ea85021adb9" :step/math-academy-id 45599 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097653}}] :activity/title "Calculating Areas Bounded by Parametric Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096851}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167733} :activity/id #uuid "193c6c55-9c5f-534c-a4fd-3813e9a5ca50" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050888 :topic/id #uuid "6e5e65de-23e7-594c-bf7e-0dbcc93f4656" :topic/math-academy-id 3261} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167630 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166795 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166633 :question/id #uuid "2281e86d-4f21-53da-b6fa-1f178d4862a5" :question/math-academy-id "e-8433"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1df2e164-251d-5ea1-a49a-fe5bc8d67ae6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048878}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167409}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Function Using the Hyperbolic Pythagorean Identity"} :step/id #uuid "e99f7bb7-2c5d-59b4-aebb-4a77afde4a43" :step/math-academy-id 31414 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167734}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167631 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166796 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166634 :question/id #uuid "9b63d35a-f1c5-554f-ac4c-68d18afa153a" :question/math-academy-id "e-8434"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "97c01757-f2bf-5b7b-b51b-ff490b54f555" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167411}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Function Using the Hyperbolic Secant-Tangent Identity"} :step/id #uuid "e2ca22ef-592a-5048-ab56-3633a1be0db3" :step/math-academy-id 31415 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167632}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167632 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166797 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166635 :question/id #uuid "7af9f5f6-1e14-55ea-8a09-25d211cb40d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-8435"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5f377f3-700a-57a9-96e5-954aed678e62" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167413}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Function Using the Hyperbolic Cosecant-Cotangent Identity"} :step/id #uuid "e260970c-9980-5233-ab9d-73344e2691fa" :step/math-academy-id 31416 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167633}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167633 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166798 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166636 :question/id #uuid "14733493-2ad9-5fc2-9afe-26384f6ce9ed" :question/math-academy-id "e-8436"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a95e37c6-66f4-5a70-87e8-31f3694a20a4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167414} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167415} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527994}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Hyperbolic Functions With Linear Arguments"} :step/id #uuid "35e1d4ff-896a-599d-bb79-ecb5cec36df6" :step/math-academy-id 31417} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167733 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168118 :tutorial/content "We can compute integrals containing hyperbolic functions using the hyperbolic counterparts of the Pythagorean identity. We use Osborn's rule to convert a trigonometric identity to a corresponding hyperbolic identity.\n\nOsborn's rule states that to convert a trigonometric identity to a corresponding hyperbolic identity, we\n\n- replace every trigonometric function with its corresponding hyperbolic function, and\n- for any product of two trigonometric sines, change the sign of the corresponding product of hyperbolic sines.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose we want to calculate the following integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\cosh^2{x} - \\sinh^2{x} \\: \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall the Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n\\cos^2{x} + \\sin^2{x} = 1\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the corresponding hyperbolic identity\n\n$$\n\\cosh^2{x} - \\sinh^2{x} = 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int\\cosh^{2} x - \\sinh^{2} x_(⏟)_(1)dx = \\int1dx = x + C\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "250a3423-70a4-5d38-9b0f-6f801a0479d1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5553 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1283c2a5-d791-5099-bd72-546a74c8f4b8" :step/math-academy-id 34427 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167630}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167734 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168119 :tutorial/content "$$\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\nWe can use Osborn's rule to write down the hyperbolic equivalents of the sectant-tangent and cotangent-cosecant identities.\n\nFrom the secant-tangent identity\n\n$$\n1 + \\tan^2 x = \\sec^2 x\n$$\n\nwe have the equivalent hyperbolic identity\n\n$$\n1 - \\tanh^2 x = \\sech^2 x\n$$\n\nSimilarly, from the cotangent-cosecant identity\n\n$$\n1+\\cot^2 x = \\csc^2 x\n$$\n\nwe have the equivalent hyperbolic identity\n\n$$\n1-\\coth^2 x = -\\csch^2 x\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n\\coth^2 x - 1 = \\csch^2 x\n$$\n\nThese identities can be used to solve certain integrals involving the expressions $\\tanh^2{x}$, $\\coth^2 x$, $\\sech^2 x$, or $\\csch^2{x}$." :tutorial/id #uuid "525b90c4-f6ea-5c55-8602-49547e261df1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5554 :tutorial/title "Calculating Integrals Using Related Hyperbolic Identities"} :step/id #uuid "ec6b416a-7645-5f7b-b41d-c553c9f1a62b" :step/math-academy-id 34428 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167631}}] :activity/title "Integration Using the Hyperbolic Pythagorean Identities" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186167027}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084294} :activity/id #uuid "50780a4d-0efe-5dba-905f-c67e5889db4b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050957 :topic/id #uuid "755ddd1f-5d88-5a57-8c26-e4a1505b3fff" :topic/math-academy-id 749} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084294 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084492 :tutorial/content "When the $n$th term $a_n$ of an infinite series involves $n$th powers, we can determine whether the series converges or diverges using the **root test**.\n\nSuppose we have a series\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n =1} ^\\infty a_n\n$$\n\nsuch that\n\n$$\na_n \\geq 0\n$$\n\nfor all\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\n. We define $L$ as the following limit:\n\n$$\nL = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty}\\sqrt [n] {a_n} = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty}\\left({a_n}\\right)^{1/n}\n$$\n\nThen, the root test states the following:\n\n- if $L < 1$, the series converges.\n- if $L >1$ or $L = \\infty$, the series diverges.\n- if $L = 1$, the root test is inconclusive.\n\nLet's use the root test to show that the following geometric series converges:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac {1} {2^n}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our sequence $a_n$ is defined as\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{1}{2^n}, \\qquad n\\geq 1\n$$\n\nTo apply the root test, we calculate the limit of $\\sqrt[n]{a_n}$ as $n\\to\\infty{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= lim_(n → ∞)\\sqrt[n]{\\frac{1}{2^{n}}} \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞)\\frac{\\sqrt[n]{1}}{\\sqrt[n]{2^{n}}} \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞)\\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nL=\\dfrac12 < 1\n$$\n\nour series converges by the root test.\n\nFinally, when applying the root test, it's worth bearing the following limit facts in mind:\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\sqrt [n] {a} = 1$ for $a > 0$\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\sqrt [n] {n} = 1$\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\sqrt [n] {\\dfrac 1 {n!}} = 0$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3a3259b2-b4b5-580c-a5f2-d1718c65a022" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6116 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6c859a87-7f6f-521b-9bcf-5d8691ae2dbc" :step/math-academy-id 37147 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084295}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084295 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083340 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083143 :question/id #uuid "c0a47f18-97ca-5bbe-8825-f088318a4cb4" :question/math-academy-id "e-9875"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "469975d9-f2a2-5e3e-9f26-cee3557cb88a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050706}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083527} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083823}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Root Test to a Convergent Series"} :step/id #uuid "9c652735-cd8b-57c0-9f3a-55d6a813833a" :step/math-academy-id 37149 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084296}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084296 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083341 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083144 :question/id #uuid "031d607b-0478-5676-ac4b-596431ca9481" :question/math-academy-id "e-9876"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cab29074-3281-5c02-9a2b-423077675aef" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083539} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083831} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233443}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Root Test in Divergent and Inconclusive Cases"} :step/id #uuid "b30928b4-83ea-54cf-be19-db7f024517fb" :step/math-academy-id 37150}] :activity/title "The Root Test" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186083440}]}
